## Solving the paradox

How can each German patient be at lower risk, while Germany as a whole has more infections? The most likely answer is simple: Germans go into hospital more often. In 2011, there were about 246 hospital stays for every 1,000 people in Germany, compared with 165 in the EU/EEA (@tbl-paradox). Germany has more hospital beds per person than any other European country, and it has been argued that more beds lead to more people being admitted, including some who would not really need to be there. Each stay is slightly safer (2.4 HAIs per 100 stays, against 2.8 in the EU/EEA), but there are many more of them, and the infections add up.


::: {#tbl-paradox .cell tbl-cap='Each hospital stay in Germany is less likely to lead to an infection, but Germans go into hospital much more often, so Germany has more infections per person than the EU/EEA average (2011–2012). Best estimate, with the likely range in brackets.'}
::: {.cell-output-display}

